e call case endings but that's still  you know i write with a pen  and so the with the pen forces the pen  the word for pen changes that's a case  ending but it's still these connections  between the verb and the noun in  japanese the preposition actually comes  after  so instead of being to the store it's  store two but the same principle you've  got to connect these verbs to the nouns  and that's all there is  and so um when you have uh  phrases which are the key  you know element that i you got to start  noticing what's happening in this phrase  the phrase might just be  a connecting word with a noun  or it might be a verb a connecting word  and a noun  and when i read languages i say what i  think is worthwhile saving what i think  helps me notice the language there's no  hard and fast rule you can say the whole  sentence or you can say short phrases  and i'm going to give you an example of  that from our many stories and i think  the many stories are a great place to do  this noticing because  if you have long sentences with many  many phrases it's more difficult to  notice these phrases as i'll demonstrate  in the many stories the sentences are  very short typically two maybe three  sometimes one phrase in the sentence but  first of all let me tell you that in so  far as the many stories are concerned  we're a little bit behind we're relying  on our volunteers  but we have  60 stories in english basically or 50  let's say in english 50 in greek  we have 20 in russian 10 in arabic 10 in  swedish 10 in german five in italian  come along  we have 20 in european spanish 20 in  korean  we have 15 in french we have five in  esperanto  we have 15 in dutch 10 in chinese 10 in  polish 20 or no we have 50 in select  but we have a bunch more that are almost  there belarusian we have 10 danish farsi  finnish hebrew  polish  japanese are working on ukrainian we're  working on portuguese romanian we're  working on thai turkish and we've got  people saying they're going to h